// array2_ext.strategies -- 2D-array strategy rules that the shipped
// array2.strategies / array2_char.strategies leave uncovered.
//
// The 1D mixed/undef rules (IntArray::mixed_full, mixed_missing_i,
// undef_missing_i, ...) are built into the driver and are complete.  What is
// missing is the 2D layer, so every rule here only peels or restores the OUTER
// (row) level and lets the builtin 1D rules finish the element access.
//
//   A  tolerant row fold        Array2::missing_i * Array::full(row)
//                                 -> Array2::full(replace_row i row rows)
//      CharArray2 only: upstream array2.strategies ids 10/11/12 (post(2))
//      already do this for IntArray2, and int_array id 31 stops a pure read
//      from rewriting the row in the first place.
//      The shipped ids 2/8/9 only fold a row that is still syntactically
//      rows[i].  After a cell access the 1D rules have rewritten the row into
//      replace_Znth j (Znth j rows[i]) rows[i], so those rules no longer match
//      and the fold falls through to a manual VC.  These run at post(3) so the
//      shipped exact-match rules keep priority.
//
//   B  mixed 2D cell access      Array2::mixed_full -> Array2::mixed_missing_i
//                                 * Array::mixed_missing_i(row)
//      Peels both levels in one step, mirroring the shipped ids 1/6/7.  The
//      accessed value is reported as Array2::mixed_val(rows[i], j) and the
//      cell's definedness as the obligation Array2::mixed_def(rows[i], j) --
//      the engine needs the value as an equation it can solve for, and QCP's
//      strategy DSL cannot spell `Znth j rows[i] None == Some v` directly
//      (only a bare identifier is accepted in a {..} type instantiation).
//      Both helpers are plain definitions in array2_ext_lib.v.
//
//   BW mixed 2D cell WRITE       Array2::mixed_full -> Array2::mixed_missing_i
//                                 * Array::mixed_missing_i(row)
//      Same peel as B but triggered by an `undef_data_at` need and carrying NO
//      definedness obligation -- a write must not require the cell to already
//      hold a value.  Without this rule B captures writes too and emits an
//      unprovable Array2::mixed_def obligation on every store.
//
//   C  mixed 2D row fold        Array2::mixed_missing_i * Array::mixed_full(row)
//                                 -> Array2::mixed_full(replace_mixed_row ...)
//
//   D  undef 2D row split       Array2::undef_full -> Array::undef_full(row)
//                                 * Array2::undef_missing_i
//      Lets the first write into an uninitialized 2D array reach the builtin
//      1D undef rules.  There is deliberately no fold rule back out of
//      undef_missing_i: that needs a heterogeneous "some rows written, some
//      still undef" predicate, which the separation-logic library does not
//      define yet.
//
//   E  mixed/full -> undef weakening (forgetting a 2D array's contents), and
//      mixed -> full once every cell has been written (Array2::some_rows l is
//      map (map Some) l).  The latter is how a partially-initialized array
//      leaves the mixed world; the 1D library has mixed_full_to_full, the 2D
//      functor does not, so array2_ext_lib.v proves it.
//
// Address forms mirror the shipped files: p + i*m*sizeof(T) + j*sizeof(T),
// pointer_offset(p, i*m + j, T), and pointer_offset(pointer_offset(p,i,T[m]),j,T).
//
// Every rule is discharged in
//   Rocq/examples/LLM_bench/Codeforces/array2_ext_strategy_proof.v

#include "array2_ext_def.h"

id : 1
priority : core(1)
left : IntArray2::mixed_full(?p, ?n, ?m, ?rows) at 0
right : data_at((?p + ((?i * ?m) * sizeof(I32))) + (?j * sizeof(I32)), I32, ?v) at 1
check : infer(0 <= i);
        infer(i < n);
        infer(0 <= j);
        infer(j < m);
action : right_erase(1);
         left_erase(0);
         left_add(IntArray2::mixed_missing_i(p, i, 0, n, m, rows));
         left_add(IntArray::mixed_missing_i(p + (i * m * sizeof(I32)), j, 0, m, rows[i]));
         right_add(v == Array2::mixed_val(rows[i], j));
         right_add(Array2::mixed_def(rows[i], j));

